module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CeilingNotStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND AT rung 1, mitigatable per site: an author can rewrite one recursion into the intercepted
  // fold and that site stops consuming stack (three sites were, in gunbc#15258). The CEILING is
  // deliberately not stated here because it is not this row's to state: removing the cost class
  // entirely is a native-execution / interpreter-retirement question, which is another manager's
  // authority (Manager 2, native execution). So the row is filed unread on the ceiling rather than
  // guessing one, and the routing ask is recorded in the last receipt.
  rung: RungUnread { cause: CeilingNotStated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a `.dag` function whose recursion count grows with the size of its input runs on
     the v1 interpreter, and the interpreter gives every interpreted call a host frame. Call depth is
     therefore host stack depth. Nothing in the source says so: the function parses, type-checks, and
     is correct on every small input, and it fails only when some consumer hands it a big one. The
     failure can also arrive as someone else's death, because the recursion is a property of the
     CALLEE's shape and the frame belongs to whoever's witness happens to be running.",

    "WHY IT LOOKS SAFE AND ISN'T, and this is the instructive part. Tail position is not a mitigation
     HERE. The v1 interpreter performs NO tail-call optimization: `tco`, `tail call` and `trampoline`
     appear nowhere in src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs, and the only interpreted functions the
     interpreter realizes natively at all are fold_list and fold_list_right (arms
     `native_intercept.fold_list`, `native_intercept.fold_list_right`). `__tco_loop` -- the marker that
     makes people believe the recursion is handled -- is produced by the EMITTER
     (is_tco_eligible / emit_shared_tco_expr in src/v1/05_emit.dag) and exists only in generated Rust.
     So a function can carry a __tco_loop in its mirror, be genuinely O(1) on the native lane, and
     still put one host frame per element on the wet lane, which is what `claim_batch --claim-run`
     executes. Checking the emitted Rust proves the native lane; it says nothing about the interpreted one.",

    "TWO REPORTING SURFACES FOR THE ONE CAUSE, which is why this class reads as two unrelated bugs.
     Below the interpreter's own ceiling the recursion is invisible. Cross the ceiling and the wet lane
     refuses LOUDLY with a typed runtime error that names the function (`call depth exceeded ... at fn
     '<name>' ... refused, never a host stack overflow`). Cross it on a path the ceiling does not
     intercept and the process dies SILENTLY by host stack overflow (rc=134, `thread 'main' has
     overflowed its stack`), naming nothing -- and the claim that dies is whatever witness was running,
     not the function at fault. Both were observed in gunbc#15258 on two shapes of the same defect: a
     list comparator refused by name at its ceiling, while a census witness that merely imported a long
     list aborted on the host stack after hours.",

    "HOW THE FAULTY FRAME WAS FOUND, since the crash names nothing. The before-receipt for the host
     abort was 13,737 identical host frames (call_function -> call_function_dispatch -> call_function_inner
     -> eval_expr -> eval_pure_named_call), which identifies the interpreter but not the .dag function.
     The instrument that does name it is a call-depth trace over call_function_dispatch that prints the
     callee's own symbol at each new maximum depth (a temporary print in a scratch copy; the interpreter
     has no such hook today). It showed depth climbing by a FIXED STEP per sibling through a mutual
     family -- a pair comparator calling two node comparison functions calling it back -- which is the
     signature of a frame held across a recursive call rather than a list walked once. Corollary the
     trace forces on any classifier: a +N-per-iteration pattern with N > 1 is a MUTUAL family, so
     single-function scanning under-reports this class structurally. Reading the fix's shape off
     `tail_position` alone is likewise wrong in this lane: what costs a frame is a call whose result is
     still needed -- inside `&&` or `||`, a constructor field, an argument, a comparison -- AND, because
     there is no tail-call optimization, a pure self-tail loop too. The last of these is what makes the
     population big: tail-recursive walkers written in the good style are still charged.",

    "THE POPULATION, sized rather than guessed. A source scan over dag/ and src/v2/ for self-recursive
     call sites in a frame-holding position found 1,726 sites across 1,248 distinct functions; adding
     the pure-self-tail shape the wet lane also charges raises the population further. The census table
     (top sites by count, with the statement text) is in the gunbc#15258 PR body. This row does NOT
     promise to rewrite them: per-site rewrites are rung 1 work and were taken only where a named
     consumer was blocked. The list is the population, not a to-do; the count is the finding.",

    "WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. No site here is proven reachable with an input large enough to die -- most
     are never called with anything long. The claim is about the COST MODEL (depth is unpriced and
     charged to a shared host stack, so the failure is deferred to whichever consumer crosses the line)
     and about the two surfaces that make it unattributable. A site with no large-input consumer is not
     a defect waiting to be fixed; it is why the class goes unnoticed.",

    "CEILING AND TRIGGER, deliberately left to their owner. The per-site mitigation exists and works
     (rewrite the walk through the intercepted fold). The class only disappears when the wet lane stops
     being the execution surface, i.e. under native execution / interpreter retirement -- Manager 2's
     authority. Filing the row with its ceiling unstated is the honest state, and routing it there is
     recorded as the ask rather than assumed.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "zip_eq"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "list_init"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.dag", decl_name: "dag_string_framed_body_optional"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.test.claim.algebra.list_linear_combinators", decl_name: "zip_eq_compares_a_list_too_long_to_recurse_over"),
  ],
}
